\description{
Matched SNP and microsatellite genotypes for 20 retained diploid animals in
each of 24 hypothetical forest ponds on a real southeastern Ohio landscape.
The neutral SLiM simulation was motivated by a forest pond-breeding salamander.
Pond locations, hydroperiods, demographic parameters, and genetic data are
simulated. Elevation and land cover are derived from public USGS products.
}
\details{
Call \code{terra::unwrap()} on the two packed raster elements before use.
No downloads or simulation software are needed to analyze the bundled data.
All retained individuals have both marker panels from the same ancestry.
Screening removes known immediate pedigree relationships within ponds;
this is not a claim that the retained animals are unrelated at deeper
generations or that such relationships would be known in field samples.

Ponds were screened for native forest, at least 90 percent forest within
100 m, and at least 60 percent within 300 m. These are hypothetical design
rules, not estimated habitat thresholds. Exchange has strong reproductive
philopatry with a long tail among ponds. The exchange model uses endpoints
on a resistance graph; it does not simulate observed animal travel paths.

The SNP panel was filtered for pooled retained-sample minor allele frequency
of at least 0.05. Locus spacing does not establish independent loci. The
effective degrees of freedom used in a Wishart example is an assumption,
distinct from the SNP count. Spatial validation conditions on this globally
ascertained panel, rather than validating marker discovery itself.

The source history was specified before fitting. Other histories and SNP
panel sizes were checked during development; the bundled history was not
selected for the strongest fit. The simulation illustrates an estimable
mechanism, not calibration or validation of a species-specific demographic
model. See the installed provenance file for sources and processing details.
}
